cff-version: 1.2.0
message: |-
  Preprint. Version 1.1.0. Text: CC BY 4.0.
type: software
title: "Fault-tolerant quantum computation on a line from one fixed translation-invariant rule"
version: 1.1.0
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24357734"
date-released: '2026-11-22'
license: CC-BY-4.0
authors: [{"family-names": "Douglas", "given-names": "Seth", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4708-3252"}, {"family-names": "Mghirbi", "given-names": "Nidhal", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6534-1118"}]
keywords: ["quantum fault tolerance", "translation-invariant quantum circuits", "one-dimensional quantum computation", "noisy classical control", "self-simulating cellular automata", "quantum cellular automata", "coherent errors"]
abstract: "One fixed finite-range, translation-invariant, time-periodic quantum circuit on a one-dimensional ring samples the computational-basis output of any width-n, depth-D Toffoli/Hadamard circuit on the all-zero input within total variation error ε. The ring size and number of periods are at most A(n+D+log₂(1/ε))^A for a fixed A, and the per-site register is fixed. The polynomial-time computable product preparation puts the program in classical basis registers and initializes all quantum registers to zero; a deterministic polynomial-time classical decoder uses only n, D and a sufficient integer precision request. Independent wire depolarization or whole-site replacement acts after every elementary layer, including on classical control, below a fixed positive threshold. Theorem D extends the accuracy and polynomial resource bounds to sufficiently small general local fault-path noise, including coherent errors and correlated bath interactions satisfying a fault-path superset bound. Its threshold is positive but astronomically small. The proof combines exact recovery on certified supports, multiscale dense-witness counting for overlapping long tests, and the established fault-path superset method. Noiseless recursive encoding and recovery give the corresponding reference-entangled input/output guarantee. An elementary register-bank corollary replaces the periodic word by one fixed channel at every step, preserves noise after every original layer, and stores a reference-entangled qubit with fidelity at least 15/16 through a horizon linear in ring size. Its ideal onsite bank shift is a noiseless timing structure; noise within a decomposition of that shift and times beyond the horizon are not covered. The construction follows the one-dimensional route proposed by Dünnweber, Styliaris and Trivedi for their two-dimensional construction, which already treats general local fault-path noise with polylogarithmic overhead. Our overhead is polynomial, with very large constants and an initialized synchronous hierarchy."
